a9c8e22b0b33"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which functions are belonging to $C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$?","options":["The function $f(x) = |x|$ only","Only discontinuous functions","Only functions with no derivatives","Polynomial functions"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ec28cf185a2191c631017ae5"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"To which differentiability class does $f(x)=|x|$ belong?","options":["$f(x) = |x|$ belongs to $C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$.","$f(x) = |x|$ is not in $C^0(\\mathbb{R})$.","$f(x) = |x|$ is differentiable everywhere on $\\mathbb{R}$.","$f(x) = |x|$ belongs to $C^0(\\mathbb{R})$ but not to $C^1(\\mathbb{R})$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0c50f834af6e44e4540bb888"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement describes the relation between $C^{k+1}$ and $C^k$?","options":["$C^k$ is contained in $C^{k+1}$ for every function.","$C^{k+1}$ is contained in $C^k$.","$C^{k+1}$ and $C^k$ have the same functions only when $k=0$.","$C^{k+1}$ contains exactly the polynomial functions."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"81bd06401fb5d966f2cecc2a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $C^0$ contain?","options":["Only polynomial functions","Only functions with no values","Only functions with a second derivative","All continuous functions"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dc1be15c61599b0291842adb"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f\\in C^2(X)$, which statement is supported by the definition?","options":["$f''$ exists but $f'$ need not exist.","$f$ is not continuous on $X$.","$f'$ and $f''$ exist and are continuous on $X$.","$f$ has derivatives only outside $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fab20ab852dd3964b9afba45"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f\\in C^3(X)$, which conclusion follows from the containment result?","options":["$f\\notin C^2(X)$","$f$ is uncountable","$f$ has no first derivative","$f\\in C^1(X)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e20bed3d7047c09d5ef95e4f"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which condition is not enough to guarantee $f\\in C^1(X)$?","options":["$f'$ exists and is continuous on $X$.","$f$ is a polynomial on $\\mathbb{R}$.","$f'$ exists on $X$ but is not continuous on $X$.","$f$ is smooth on $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f3fbfacdbfa4c4659769b6c"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are higher differentiability classes stronger?","options":["They require fewer derivatives to exist.","They require differentiability but remove continuity of derivatives.","They apply only to polynomials.","They require more derivatives to exist and be continuous."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"347e1b0dabbfbca574b37ee1"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_differentiability_class_60b39faf_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which classification matches $|x|$ at $0$?","options":["It must be in $C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$.","It cannot be in $C^0(\\mathbb{R})$, which is false","It must be in $C^2(\\mathbb{R})$.","It can be in $C^0(\\mathbb{R})$ but not in $C^1(\\mathbb{R})$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8382128598892c6f2542b9c3