expansion_pack_015_differentiability_class_new_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f$ is smooth on $X$, which statement follows?","options":["$f\\notin C^0(X)$.","$f$ has no derivatives.","$f\\in C^k(X)$ for every relevant $k$.","$f$ is discontinuous everywhere."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a3a7f0504e08c07c144fefc2"},{"id":"expansion_pack_015_differentiability_class_new_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A polynomial function on $\\mathbb{R}$ belongs to which class?","options":["Only $C^0(\\mathbb{R})$ and not $C^1(\\mathbb{R})$","No differentiability class","$C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$","Only outside $\\mathbb{R}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"55758bdf5b1f83e2ee93a387"},{"id":"expansion_pack_015_differentiability_class_new_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is $C^{k+1}$ a stronger condition than $C^k$?","options":["It requires one fewer derivative.","It drops the continuity requirement for the $k$-th derivative.","It requires one more continuous derivative.","It is equivalent to $C^k$ for non-polynomial functions."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6d675e66b3523bb5ca4157bd"},{"id":"expansion_pack_015_differentiability_class_new_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $f\\in C^\\infty(X)$, which conclusion is valid?","options":["$f\\notin C^1(X)$","$f$ has no second derivative","$f\\in C^2(X)$","$f$ is not continuous"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef8956a645507795344cd3df"}]}}